{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "section_title", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\section{", "suffix": "}\n\\label{section-fibre-products}"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:38021e2a851ce2feba5a95dd582f1687d37e49c3b08ed100b5688a27d67fa3fe", "source_text": "Fibre products of schemes", "source_text_hash": "sha256:62e2287a4a5620524c345879f3bacee50bac16664ee1911819b8f1dfe4f4b16a", "unit_id": "tag:01JO:title"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:39a8300456f97d8c2ba04e0e7a4b06d0f7f95242ac8580d29d8729bcf816d802", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nHere is a review of the general definition, even though\nwe have already shown that fibre products of schemes exist.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b475e6daad16543a5044a3df1b6fb831142856b725da4470d7af3b73680263e6", "unit_id": "tag:01JO:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "definition", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : X \\to S$", "MATH_0002": "$g : Y \\to S$", "MATH_0003": "$X \\times_S Y$", "MATH_0004": "$p : X \\times_S Y \\to X$", "MATH_0005": "$q : X \\times_S Y \\to Y$", "MATH_0006": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\nX \\times_S Y \\ar[r]_q \\ar[d]_p & Y \\ar[d]^g \\\\\nX \\ar[r]^f & S\n}\n$$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{categories-definition-fibre-products}", "TEXTITCLOSE_0001": "}", "TEXTITOPEN_0001": "{\\it "}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{definition}\n\\label{definition-fibre-product}\n", "suffix": "\\end{definition}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:51673cd3c421281301dd473970130ad467c7bad3b17c33d22665ed5f669fe5f2", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:22715d0816efa92b6e1821c0c271540f85e922523c5bce8e5cda32a8b83dce21", "source_text": "Given morphisms of schemes <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002>\nthe <TEXTITOPEN_0001>fibre product<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> is a scheme <MATH_0003> together\nwith projection morphisms <MATH_0004>\nand <MATH_0005> sitting into the following\ncommutative diagram\n<MATH_0006>\nwhich is universal among all diagrams of this sort,\nsee Categories, Definition <REF_0001>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:766dfdc427a19bffdb16b4fa61a34c2aa84eae04db9f50e15f47ca4830ee14ab", "unit_id": "tag:01JP:statement"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$$\n\\xymatrix{\nT \\ar[rrrd] \\ar@{-->}[rrd] \\ar[rrdd]\n&\n&\n\\\\\n&\n&\nX \\times_S Y \\ar[d] \\ar[r]\n&\nY \\ar[d]\n\\\\\n&\n&\nX \\ar[r]\n&\nS\n}\n$$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:e027e134a84d3dcfa15cf15aeefa713c596b8073fecd76a84d82d257e892ef78", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:53329c46f05facfe951a0ce3ff7bc9aa6fe670758c9b1faa24ff291daa61658f", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn other words, given any solid commutative diagram of\nmorphisms of schemes\n<MATH_0001>\nthere exists a unique dotted arrow making the diagram commute.\nWe will prove some lemmas which will tell us how to think about\nfibre products.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:4680d0a325d3d2c265c70b1fc6a84d36fd1c0d6bfe9909950c64fb7604e9c124", "unit_id": "tag:01JO:prose-0002"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : X \\to S$", "MATH_0002": "$g : Y \\to S$", "MATH_0003": "$X, Y, S$", "MATH_0004": "$X \\times_S Y$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-fibre-product-affines}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:4d395c08ca72826127a267a43f1dd69d8bdbc72e3116867a6ba7bff794487275", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:063ddeeda59438e296dd7c409d479d5f8d67c891cd06bd9e7f6cd35889957b28", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> be morphisms of schemes\nwith the same target. If <MATH_0003> are all affine then\n<MATH_0004> is affine.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:ac460a11d3da362fe2a4b5ac81f81037120bdafa55534ec8f7376ebce6cb4c7f", "unit_id": "tag:01JQ:statement"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "proof", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X = \\Spec(A)$", "MATH_0002": "$Y = \\Spec(B)$", "MATH_0003": "$S = \\Spec(R)$", "MATH_0004": "$\\Spec(A \\otimes_R B)$", "MATH_0005": "$X \\times_S Y$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-fibre-product-affine-schemes}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{proof}\n", "suffix": "\\end{proof}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:4ea8ee216d0b21e033b7ae2d5cf9a36c6ff2d5b10d5b0e1cb815d6c0ff7b8662", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:40398cc97e7b1d6ed3fd834647b4a6cc8130acd0a5b0633b99adc0f5bbd62bea", "source_text": "Suppose that <MATH_0001>, <MATH_0002>\nand <MATH_0003>. By Lemma <REF_0001>\nthe affine scheme <MATH_0004>\nis the fibre product <MATH_0005> in the category\nof locally ringed spaces. Hence it is a fortiori the\nfibre product in the category of schemes.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:de39883e4b59d78d940f5493417b4f32e556df8bc7044e03cb2e0296ac8c7cd1", "unit_id": "tag:01JQ:proof"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "01JO", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$f : X \\to S$", "MATH_0002": "$g : Y \\to S$", "MATH_0003": "$X \\times_S Y$", "MATH_0004": "$p$", "MATH_0005": "$q$", "MATH_0006": "$U \\subset S$", "MATH_0007": "$V \\subset X$", "MATH_0008": "$W \\subset Y$", "MATH_0009": "$f(V) \\subset U$", "MATH_0010": "$g(W) \\subset U$", "MATH_0011": "$V \\times_U W \\to X \\times_S Y$", "MATH_0012": "$V \\times_U W$", "MATH_0013": "$p^{-1}(V) \\cap q^{-1}(W)$"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-open-fibre-product}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:cc24167e5540ef98ad531cd69db37cc887f37c2e74ebab67db549ce6403da231", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:05d0ee29696c715104f5a0d286963ff1f6beb6993b3a046af6c81b20b1bc8d26", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> and <MATH_0002> be morphisms of schemes\nwith the same target. Let <MATH_0003>, <MATH_0004>, <MATH_0005> be the fibre product.\nSuppose that <MATH_0006>,\n<MATH_0007>, <MATH_0008> are open subschemes\nsuch that <MATH_0009> and <MATH_0010>.\nThen the canonical morphism\n<MATH_0011> is an open immersion\nwhich identifies <MATH_0012> with <MATH_0013>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:e56d38ae5699c11552d09f4a898dd399376ef3da4d75f9c3ac85b057f6cb0909", "unit_id": "tag:01JR:statement"}
